  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 240 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nBy Sun morning the Bermuda ridge centered off the GA/SC coast will \nhave weakened enough to allow a backdoor cold front to move SW out \nof the Carolinas and into NE GA Sun afternoon. This front is fairly \nweak and looses a lot of its strength as it pushes into the old \nweakening ridge, but it will be a catalyst for increased precip \nchances Sun/Mon. Instability indices stay fairly weak as well so not \nexpecting much in the way of sever storms Sun afternoon/evening. \nWould not be surprised to see a strong storm or two as we are still \nin summer convection season. Slightly cooler air moves into the area \nbehind the frontal boundary Monday, as a ridge of high pressure \ncentered over Hudson Bay sinks southward into the Mid-Atlantic \nstates. This ridge sets up like a wedge down the eastern seaboard \nwith cooler easterly flow sting up across GA and the Carolinas \nMon/Tue. There still remains the potential for diurnal convection, \nMon, with things drying out a bit for Tue/Wed/Thu. \n\nLittle change in the temperature trends are likely through the \nweekend with temps in the middle to upper 90s through Sunday. Will \nsee slightly cooler temps Mon/Tue/Wed after the frontal boundary \npasses through with highs in the 80s to lower 90s. Temps rise back \ninto the middle 90s by the end of next week.&&",
